  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "zoom-axis",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 1440,
      "text": "One zoom control, four representations \u2014 not a scale transform.\nL2 Card \u2192 L1 Grid \u2192 L0 Constellation. Both panes always move together, so \"spread everything out in front of me\" is one gesture."
    },
    {
      "id": "l0-note",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": 3120,
      "y": 950,
      "w": 460,
      "text": "L0 colours every card in the pool by combo degree \u2014 a heat map of where your synergies actually are. Nothing is filtered or sampled away: every candidate has a pip."
    },
    {
      "id": "insp-note",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": 3120,
      "y": 1020,
      "w": 440,
      "text": "Every recommendation states its case. Kiki-Jiki's three combos share a piece and still count as three \u2014 distinct combos, per the domain rule."
    },
    {
      "id": "mobile-note",
      "page": "page-2",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 900,
      "text": "Same product, not a reduced one. The deck is a bottom sheet at three detents so both regions stay on screen; swipe accepts and rejects, and every swipe has a tap equivalent in the inspect sheet."
    },
    {
      "id": "switch-note",
      "page": "page-2",
      "x": 2150,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 420,
      "text": "There is no save button anywhere in this design, so switching decks never prompts. The chip in the command bar is the switcher."
    },
    {
      "id": "query-note",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": 4680,
      "y": 950,
      "w": 520,
      "text": "A Scryfall-style query narrows the pool before grouping \u2014 the groups still form, over fewer cards.\nFiltering never silently hides a good card: each group reports what it withheld, and accepted cards dim rather than disappear."
    },
    {
      "id": "arch-note",
      "page": "page-1",
      "x": 1560,
      "y": 2040,
      "w": 520,
      "text": "Archetype is what makes composition targets mean anything \u2014 an aggro deck and a control deck do not want the same numbers.\nIt is suggested from the commander's own statistics rather than asked blind, and switching it later moves targets without touching a single card."
    }
  ],
